MAJOR_VERSION=5
STANDARD_VERSION=1
RELEASE_VERSION=0
DESCRIPTION<<PORTFOLIO_NOTIFY_DESC_END
5.1.0: Flintstone now tells the portfolio when it has published, instead of the portfolio having to be told by hand.

Adds .github/workflows/notify-portfolio.yml, which sends a repository_dispatch event of type project-updated to BPForbes/Bailey-Forbes-Portfolio-Site.github.io so bailey-forbes.com re-queries GitHub, regenerates the Flintstone metadata it displays (version, commit and pull request counts, Linguist language balance, and release milestones read from version/locked/*.ver), and redeploys. No Flintstone state is sent in the payload: the event carries only the repository, commit, ref, workflow name, and project id, and the portfolio remains responsible for deriving every number it prints. Nothing about the kernel, the build, or the promotion gates changes.

Timing is fail-closed against the existing promotion path. The workflow waits on Browser kernel artifact completing on main, but that workflow concluding successfully is not by itself evidence that anything was published: the deploy job is conditional on bootable, browser_compatible, and boot_smoke_passed, so a candidate that builds and tests cleanly while failing a promotion gate records a blocked outcome, leaves validate green, and skips deploy, leaving the run green with the lab unchanged. A gate job therefore reads back what the job named "Publish validated lab to GitHub Pages" actually concluded for that run, through repos/{repo}/actions/runs/{id}/jobs, and the notification is sent only on success. A skipped or failed publish stops it, so the portfolio can never advertise a lab revision that visitors cannot open.

The other workflows are deliberately not wired: CI runs on develop and feature branches and validates versioning rather than publishing, Version lock on merge pushes to develop and produces no artifact, and Deploy is workflow_dispatch only with no Pages state.

Least privilege throughout. The gate job takes actions: read and nothing else; the notify job calls a reusable workflow in the portfolio repository that declares permissions {} because the dispatch is an outbound call. Authentication is a new repository secret, PORTFOLIO_DISPATCH_TOKEN, holding a fine-grained token scoped to the single portfolio repository with Contents write, which is the narrowest permission the dispatch endpoint accepts. It is read from the environment rather than interpolated into a command line, is never echoed, and reaches no other repository. The dispatch implementation is shared rather than copied per project, so the Flintstone-side configuration is the trigger, the gate, and the call.

project-metadata.json also gains a curated releases[] section. timeline stays comprehensive and automatic (every merged pull request, every published GitHub Release); releases is the opposite on purpose: it is never derived from commits, tags, or the version/*.ver train, only ever republished from a new metadata/releases.json that a person maintains by hand. Git history is too granular and inconsistent for a portfolio to present directly, so this repository now explicitly states which releases are portfolio-worthy instead of leaving that judgment to whichever consumer reads the timeline. A curator writes only version, startDate, endDate (nullable), summary, and description; the generator computes a stable id slug from the version string and a url defaulting to the exact validated commit's version/locked directory, so "View release" always points at real, reviewable source. Eleven releases spanning 2.2.4 through 5.0.0 are curated at launch: the ten migrated from the prose already on the portfolio's Flintstone project page, plus 5.0.0 itself -- the project-metadata contract this repository already shipped on main before this PR existed, and named releases would otherwise have omitted. Validated the same way timeline is -- malformed dates, an endDate before its startDate, a duplicated version, or a non-github.com url all fail the generator outright -- and an absent or empty metadata/releases.json publishes zero named releases rather than erroring, so this ships with no workflow changes beyond the one new default argument the existing "Generate public project metadata" step already picks up.

Hand-editing metadata/releases.json is no longer the only way in. A .ver row may now carry PUBLISHED_DESCRIPTION, a single-line, portfolio-facing override for DESCRIPTION -- but only on a row that also sets GM=1; scripts/check_version_published_description_requires_gm.sh rejects it on any other row, including every root .ver, since GM=1 is already forbidden there. When promote_preproduction_for_main.sh promotes a GM=1 row to main, it now resolves the collapsed-row summary a curator would otherwise have typed by hand -- PUBLISHED_DESCRIPTION when present and non-empty, the same canonical DESCRIPTION used for the expanded body otherwise -- and upserts (matched by A.B.C, so a later promotion of the same version replaces rather than duplicates) an entry into metadata/releases.json. A GM=1 promotion is already a maintainer's "ship this" decision, so this is not a second approval gate, just one less hand-edit for the release most likely to want one; PUBLISHED_DESCRIPTION itself is never copied onto the promoted root .ver, which keeps exactly its existing MAJOR_VERSION/STANDARD_VERSION/RELEASE_VERSION/RELEASE_DATE/DESCRIPTION shape. GM=1 and PRERELEASE=1 coexisting on that same preproduction row is expected, not rejected -- every row under preproduction <A>.<B>.<C>/ already carries PRERELEASE=1 by the existing layout rule, and what actually matters -- that neither survives onto main -- is what check_version_prerelease_layout.sh and check_version_main_prerelease_policy.sh already guarantee.
PORTFOLIO_NOTIFY_DESC_END

RELEASE_DATE=2026-09-18
